<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How Much Is the Pineapple?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Create short texts based on prompts.<br>interactive: Write findings from group research projects for a presentation.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create short texts based on shopping prompts (e.g., 'I need five apples.').<br>Can write and present short findings from a group project on market items (e.g., 'Apples cost one dollar each.').<br>Can revise shopping lists or short reports for accuracy by correcting quantities and names (e.g., change 'one orange' to 'two oranges').
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e6eef4;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher shows flashcards of market items (e.g., pineapple, potatoes, cassava, apples) and asks students to quickly name them. Then, the teacher orally asks, "How much is the pineapple?" to elicit known prices (e.g., "It's two dollars."). The teacher quickly writes the names of the items on the board to visually reinforce spelling for the writing focus.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher writes a simple shopping sentence on the board: "I need two pineapples." and "How much is it?" The teacher explicitly sounds out /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money' while writing, and models writing numbers like 'one' and 'twenty' (focusing on phonemic awareness: /w/ /ʌn/, /tw/ /ɛ n/ /ti/) as part of demonstrating writing market-related words and quantities.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we listening or writing today?" (Expected answer: Writing!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write long stories or short sentences about shopping?" (Expected answer: Short sentences.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a price tag.) "Does this tell us the color or the cost?" (Expected answer: The cost/price.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A simple, short market vendor's sign displaying items and their prices (e.g., "Fresh Apples - $1 each", "Pineapple - $2", "Cassava - $3 for 2"). Genre: Informative/Transactional text. Register: Practical. Format: Written list/sign.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading and understanding simple written information about prices and quantities in a market context.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match written item names to their prices on a worksheet based on the market sign.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides True/False statements for students to write their answers: e.g., "Pineapples cost one dollar." (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students circle all the numbers (quantities and prices) they find on the market sign.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing sentences using WH-Questions ('How much is it?'), Quantities ('I need five potatoes.'), and Present Simple ('I want three apples and one banana.') with target vocabulary (market, price, shopping list, pineapple, potatoes, cassava, money, buy, need, want, cost, cheap, expensive, numbers 1-100).</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences about shopping at the market, each having one or two blanks. They write the correct target vocabulary word or number to complete the sentence. E.g., "I ____ two _______ for dinner." (need, potatoes). "How ____ is the _______?" (much, pineapple). "I want ____ apples and ____ banana." (three, one).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution (Written):</b> Teacher provides a sentence frame on the board or worksheet, such as: "I want _______ [NUMBER] _______ [ITEM] and _______ [NUMBER] _______ [ITEM]." Students then choose words from provided word banks (numbers 1-10 and market items: apples, bananas, pineapples, potatoes, cassava) to create and write at least three different shopping requests. E.g., "I want five apples and two bananas."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Written Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student creates a short "shopping list" (3-4 items with quantities) on a piece of paper. They then exchange lists. Student B reads Student A's list and writes two questions about it (e.g., "How much are the potatoes?" or "Do you need two oranges or two apples?") and checks for spelling/quantity accuracy. Student A then reads Student B's questions and writes short answers. This reinforces revising shopping lists for accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Task format:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Group writing project leading to a short, student-read presentation.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Activity: "Market Price Reporter"</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a set of picture cards depicting market items with their assigned prices (e.g., a card showing '5 apples / $3', '1 pineapple / $2', '2 cassava / $1', '4 potatoes / $2').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The task is to collaboratively write a short "Market Price Report" based on these cards. The report should describe what they "saw" at the market and the "cost" of the items.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prompt:</b> "Imagine you visited the market. Write a short report about the items you saw and their prices. Remember to use polite expressions like 'Excuse me, how much is it?' in your imagined report or presentation."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example expected output:</b> "Today at the market, I saw fresh apples. Five apples cost three dollars. A ripe pineapple costs two dollars. Two cassavas cost one dollar. I need two pineapples and six potatoes for my family."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then chooses one member to present (read) their written report to the class. This addresses "Can write and present short findings from a group project."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "Market Price Report" written collaboratively by the groups in Stage 4, and the subsequent reading of the report by a group member.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist/Observation):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (market, price, pineapple, apples, potatoes, cost, buy, need, want).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of quantities and numbers (1-100).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and organization of the written report.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic sentence structure (Subject-Verb-Object) in written sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(During presentation) Intelligible pronunciation of /p/ and /m/ and numbers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(During presentation) Use of polite expressions (e.g., 'Excuse me') if incorporated.</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a shopping list with ____ items." (Students fill in a number like '3', '5', or 'more than 5').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'How much is the [item]?' correctly: Yes / No / A little"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today about shopping at the market?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write today? What do I need more practice with?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in the writing activities (e.g., group collaboration, the cloze exercise, peer checking)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future writing lessons (e.g., more scaffolding for sentence construction, clearer guidelines for quantities)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively integrate the target grammar (WH-questions, quantities, present simple) and vocabulary into their written reports?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the writing tasks appropriately challenging for the A1.2 level?</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"In our next lesson, we will use our written market reports and shopping lists to practice summarizing information and presenting our findings, preparing for our final project on market items."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>